// A TCP connection's keepalive, as Go's net sets it: a connected or an accepted TcpClient probes its peer once the connection
// has been idle for 15 s, every 15 s, and ends the connection after 9 probes go unanswered (runtime/socket.maxon,
// connectedOptions), as Go's Dialer and ListenConfig do by default. `setKeepAlivePeriod` changes the idle time before the first
// probe, as Go's TCPConn.SetKeepAlivePeriod does, and 0 turns keepalive off, as a negative Dialer.KeepAlive does in Go. The
// interval and the count stay Go's 15 s and 9. On Windows they are TCP_KEEPINTVL and TCP_KEEPCNT, which Windows 10 sets (version
// 1709, 1703 for the count): a system that refuses them keeps its own interval and count, and the idle time is set all the same.

/// The idle time before a connection's first keepalive probe, in milliseconds, `0` meaning no keepalive. It is rounded up to a
/// whole second, as Go rounds it; 32767 s is the most Linux takes.
public typealias KeepAlivePeriodMs = int(0 to 32767000)

public extension TcpClient
	/// Probe the peer once the connection has been idle for `milliseconds`, rounded up to a whole second, and then every 15 s,
	/// ending the connection after 9 probes go unanswered; `0` turns keepalive off. Throws `connectionClosed` where the system
	/// refuses the option.
	public function setKeepAlivePeriod(mutates self, milliseconds KeepAlivePeriodMs) throws NetworkError
		try __Builtins.socketSetKeepAlive(socket.word, milliseconds as Count) otherwise 'keepAliveErr'
			throw NetworkError.connectionClosed
		end 'keepAliveErr'
	end 'setKeepAlivePeriod'
end 'TcpClient'
